 836:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** /*
 837:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** ***************************************************************************************************
 838:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *                                       POST EVENT FLAG BIT(S)
 839:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *
 840:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** * Description: This function is called to set or clear some bits in an event flag group.  The bits 
 841:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *              set or clear are specified by a 'bit mask'.
 842:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *
 843:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** * Arguments  : pgrp          is a pointer to the desired event flag group.
 844:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *
 845:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *              flags         If 'opt' (see below) is OS_FLAG_SET, each bit that is set in 'flags' w
 846:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *                            set the corresponding bit in the event flag group.  e.g. to set bits 0
 847:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *                            and 5 you would set 'flags' to:
 848:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *
 849:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *                                0x31     (note, bit 0 is least significant bit)
 850:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *
 851:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *                            If 'opt' (see below) is OS_FLAG_CLR, each bit that is set in 'flags' w
 852:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *                            CLEAR the corresponding bit in the event flag group.  e.g. to clear bi
 853:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *                            4 and 5 you would specify 'flags' as:
 854:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *
 855:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *                                0x31     (note, bit 0 is least significant bit)
 856:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *
 857:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *              opt           indicates whether the flags will be:
 858:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *                                set     (OS_FLAG_SET) or
 859:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *                                cleared (OS_FLAG_CLR)
 860:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *
 861:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *              perr          is a pointer to an error code and can be:
 862:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *                            OS_ERR_NONE                The call was successfull
 863:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *                            OS_ERR_FLAG_INVALID_PGRP   You passed a NULL pointer
 864:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *                            OS_ERR_EVENT_TYPE          You are not pointing to an event flag group
 865:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *                            OS_ERR_FLAG_INVALID_OPT    You specified an invalid option
 866:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** *
 867:./ucos/Core/os_flag.c **** * Returns    : the new value of the event flags bits that are still set.
